{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210152,"nonce":"MC3WyL80eOxfrrxUMdeVhbuG9uo=","target":"d6d97de52cbdd47f7a6af61b6baebc508800ce05933687e49ef7d7400eaad069","message":"**What is wrong.** Two directories are referenced as if they hold content and hold\nnothing:\n\n- `deploy/prometheus/` — a lone `.gitkeep`. `README.md:56` (pre-warning revision)\n  advertised \"compose, alertmanager and prometheus config\"; the current README\n  flags it as empty. There is no sample `prometheus.yml`, so nothing shows how to\n  point a Prometheus at the bundled Alertmanager that routes to oto.\n- `docs/runbooks/` — a lone `.gitkeep`. It is a documented location in the tree\n  with no content, and oto's own alerting story (the `oto_*` metrics named\n  throughout the code, e.g. `oto_thread_gap_recovered_total`, whose sustained\n  non-zero value is documented in-code as \"a channel is broken\") has no runbook.\n\n**Why it matters.** `docker-compose.yml` brings up Postgres and Alertmanager but\nnothing that fires an alert, so the fastest way to see oto do its job requires an\noperator to write the Prometheus config themselves — the difference between a\nfive-minute evaluation and an afternoon. And oto exports metrics whose meaning\nlives only in Go comments; the operator running oto in production has no\ndocumented response to any of them.\n\n**Where.** `deploy/prometheus/.gitkeep`, `docs/runbooks/.gitkeep`,\n`docker-compose.yml`, `README.md:74-78`.\n\n**Done when.** `deploy/prometheus/prometheus.yml` scrapes a sample target and\nroutes to the compose Alertmanager with a rule that fires within a minute, so\n`make db-up` plus that config demonstrates the whole path; and `docs/runbooks/`\nholds at least one page per `oto_*` alerting metric saying what it means, what to\ncheck and what to do. Empty directories that stay empty should be deleted instead.","files":null}]}